+Later KI#197.9 military-production diagnostics showed that the original 3-turn land-contact hard reject had become too restrictive as a general pre-war sanity gate. In one run Spain had 178 military units with 172 still in its own territory and almost twice Mali's power, but Mali was rejected only because estimated contact was 4 turns; Byzantium similarly had 182 military units with 172 at home and roughly four times another target's power, but an 8-turn estimate was rejected. When a separate victory-denial path bypassed the contact limit later in the same game, those large armies immediately selected farther targets and converted the wars into city captures. The problem was therefore not that approximate travel time has no strategic value, but that 3 turns made it an overriding veto before normal UWAI could weigh power, utility, closeness, diplomacy and the rest of the war context.
+
+The XML-tunable defaults are therefore now widened to 15 approximate path turns for land-reachable targets (`SAS_UWAI_WAR_TARGET_MAX_LAND_CONTACT_TURNS`) and 20 when naval transport is genuinely required (`SAS_UWAI_WAR_TARGET_MAX_SEA_CONTACT_TURNS`). This keeps a high outer sanity bound for exceptionally remote targets while allowing ordinary reachable wars to reach UWAI evaluation. The change is intentionally still a distance guard rather than deleting the safeguard entirely; future autoplay can lower or widen the values if the AI again shows either overextension or excessive reluctance.
