+## 197.9 - (Improved) Healthy AI empires could still devote too little productive capacity to offensive units even after BuildUnitProb and spending-limit fixes
+
+Screenshots/files for this issue: [google drive folder link](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1yeTrh38UwYFQDL5c-R6zDii0E4zjtDNI?usp=sharing).
+
+The continued KI#197 investigation showed that the preceding BuildUnitProb, building-gate and affordability fixes improved military catch-up but still did not guarantee that a healthy mature empire actually converted enough of its productive capacity into offensive units. Several experiments also showed why the existing military-upkeep recommendation was a poor direct target: an AI could already be far above `AI_maxUnitCostPerMil()` with only a modest army, while another economically strong empire could have large spending headroom yet continue choosing infrastructure. Fixed era/city unit quotas, simply multiplying the upkeep budget and a hard post-choice 100% unit override were therefore discarded; the latter could even replace foundational early infrastructure and weaken the economy that should support the later army.
+
+AdvCiv-SAS now instead has an XML-tunable **offensive production-capacity floor** through `SAS_AI_CHOOSE_PRODUCTION_MIN_OFFENSIVE_PRODUCTION_CAPACITY_PERCENT`. It measures the share of the empire's current **base production** assigned to `UNITAI_ATTACK` and `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY` production, so a strong hammer city contributes more than a weak new settlement and the rule scales naturally with empire size, map, era and production. The check runs only after the existing earlier Worker, Settler, explorer, panic/defense and other foundational branches, and it is disabled for financial trouble, immediate city danger, Always Peace, `AI_STRATEGY_GET_BETTER_UNITS`, culture-victory cities, non-primary areas and the existing unit-exempt cases. When the eligible empire is below the configured share, the city tries a least-represented ATTACK/ATTACK_CITY unit before opportunistic wonders and the broad later building shortcuts. Setting the define to 0 disables the feature.
+
+The initial 40% test was the first recent experiment to produce a clear broad military increase without the early-game self-sabotage of the discarded hard override. A later 100% run produced a spectacular high-pressure result, including roughly 180 combat units for both Spain and Byzantium around turn 300, but repeated testing showed that this was not a reliable scaling result. A controlled save-file 460 comparison using the same starting game at 0%, 40% and 100% showed a large improvement from 0% to 40%, but almost no additional aggregate direct combat-unit production from 40% to 100% by turn 300 (about 987 at 0%, 1340 at 40% and 1340 at 100%, excluding Barbarians and non-combat units). The 100% branch also tended to sacrifice more infrastructure/economic compounding, so stronger nominal military pressure could leave weaker production bases and did not consistently create larger late-game armies. The current default is therefore 40% as the better observed balance between military pressure and the economy that has to sustain and replace the army; 100 remains available as an intentionally extreme tuning value, while 0 disables the feature. The mechanism still does not consistently recreate the exceptional AdvCiv-SAS 5055 military-production curve, so the remaining limitation is considered a follow-up rather than hidden by an excessive default.
+
+The dedicated diagnostics added for this investigation are intentionally retained behind the existing BBAI log levels. `WAR_TARGET_SCHEME_GATE` / expanded `WAR_TARGET_EVAL` record whether a rival reaches war evaluation together with power, distance and where each side's military is located; `WAR_ATTACK_ORDER` records the low-power attacker chosen by the SAS stack-order logic and the final attacker after inherited sacrifice selection. These logs exposed that the inherited KI#61 pre-war contact limit had become too restrictive: Spain could have almost twice Mali's power with nearly all of its army at home and still hard-reject the war because estimated contact was 4 turns against a 3-turn land cap, while Byzantium could reject a much weaker target at 8 turns for the same reason. The existing victory-denial bypass demonstrated that once this hard veto was removed, the same large armies could rapidly convert into captured cities. The XML defaults are therefore now widened from 3 to 15 approximate land-contact turns and from 12 to 20 genuinely naval-contact turns. This does not force a declaration; it lets reachable targets within the wider sanity bounds proceed to normal UWAI power, utility, diplomacy, closeness and war-state evaluation. A later 40% save-file 454 test ended in a turn-342 Domination victory; the winning autoplay empire already had 106 combat units at turn 250 and 189 at turn 300, supporting the combination of stronger-but-not-maximal production pressure and fewer artificial war-opportunity vetoes.
+
+The attack-order trace still exposes a smaller separate follow-up: the SAS low-power selector can correctly choose an old/obsolete expendable unit, but inherited `AI_getBestGroupSacrifice()` may subsequently replace it. In one direct example, a 136-turn-old obsolete 0-XP Archer selected at 10% odds was replaced by a 5-turn-old 8-XP Horse Archer; across the diagnostic run, 189 logged attacks replaced an obsolete SAS-selected attacker with a non-obsolete final attacker, all through the sacrifice override. This issue remains unfixed for now and can be revisited after the broader production/war-opportunity behavior has stabilized.
+
+Improved/investigated with the help of GPT-5.6 Thinking thanks.
+
 ## 198 - (Fixed/Improved) Likely Base AdvCiv Worker/land-development underperformance, compounded by an AdvCiv-SAS Worker-demand regression and too-low minimum, could leave mature AI cities badly unimproved
 
 Primary empirical reproduction and validation in AdvCiv-SAS: save file 459.
@@ -7551,9 +7572,9 @@ To fix that mismatch without creating a second improvement-selection system, cur
 
 That regression fix was meaningful but exposed a separate and broader weakness in Worker policy. In the first corrected save-file 459 rerun, Russia improved from 39 to 26 unimproved worked plots on turn 150 and from 58 to 24 on turn 200, but it still had only 7 Workers for 6 cities on turn 200. Inspection showed that this was not another failed production gate: the old AdvCiv-SAS guaranteed primary-area minimum was effectively only 1 Worker per city plus 1 reserve for the capital area. The larger approximately 2.5 Workers-per-city formula elsewhere in Base AdvCiv was only a maximum production cap (and decayed by era), not a target or minimum. Russia reaching exactly 7 Workers for 6 primary-area cities therefore meant the old floor was being enforced; the floor itself was simply much too conservative for the importance of land development.
 
-The primary-area standing minimum is therefore now shared and XML-tunable through `SAS_AI_WORKER_PRIMARY_AREA_MINIMUM_PERCENT_PER_CITY` and `SAS_AI_WORKER_PRIMARY_AREA_MINIMUM_RESERVE`, defaulting to 200% per city plus 1 reserve (e.g. 5 cities -> 11 Workers, 6 cities -> 13 Workers). `AI_neededWorkers`, the relevant production ceilings and Worker-scrapping safeguards all respect the same minimum so one subsystem does not immediately undo another. The existing one-city early-growth protection remains: a tiny first capital is not allowed to chain-build the full multi-Worker floor before it has grown enough. Secondary areas also remain demand-driven rather than inheriting the primary-area standing force.
+The primary-area standing minimum is therefore now shared and XML-tunable through `SAS_AI_WORKER_PRIMARY_AREA_MINIMUM_PERCENT_PER_CITY` and the signed `SAS_AI_WORKER_PRIMARY_AREA_RESERVE`. The initial tuning used 200% per city plus 1 reserve, but a follow-up autoplay exposed the opposite early-game problem: once the one-city growth safeguard expired, the capital could target 3 Workers before the second Settler and delay expansion. The reserve is therefore currently -1 rather than encoding that tuning direction in the define name: positive values add Workers, 0 leaves the percentage floor unchanged and negative values reduce it. With the current defaults of 200% and -1 reserve, the standing floor is 1 Worker at 1 city, 3 at 2 cities, 5 at 3 cities, 7 at 4 cities, 9 at 5 cities and 11 at 6 cities; each additional city adds the full 2-Worker ratio without forcing a lone growing capital to carry it immediately. `AI_neededWorkers`, the relevant production ceilings and Worker-scrapping safeguards all respect the same minimum so one subsystem does not immediately undo another. The existing one-city early-growth protection remains as an additional guard against ordinary demand logic exceeding 1 Worker before the capital reaches its population threshold. Secondary areas also remain demand-driven rather than inheriting the primary-area standing force. A later full test directly confirmed the intended early progression: Russia still had only 1 Worker with 1 city on turn 30, while Sitting Bull had 3 Workers after reaching 2 cities by turn 50.
 
-The final save-file 459 validation showed a much stronger development pattern. Russia had 9 Workers for 4 cities by turn 100 and 11 Workers for 5 cities by turn 150. At turn 150, 47 of 54 worked plots were improved (only 7 unimproved), and Moscow had all 16 currently worked plots improved. By turn 200, Russia still had 11 Workers for 5 cities and 61 of 79 worked plots were improved, with Moscow at 17 of 19. The autoplay branches naturally diverged after the behavior change, so these are diagnostic comparisons rather than identical-game snapshots, but the Worker-count floor itself is directly visible and the reduction in under-improved worked land is large.
+The confirming (with the plus 1 reserve) save-file 459 run development pattern. Russia had 9 Workers for 4 cities by turn 100 and 11 Workers for 5 cities by turn 150. At turn 150, 47 of 54 worked plots were improved (only 7 unimproved), and Moscow had all 16 currently worked plots improved. By turn 200, Russia still had 11 Workers for 5 cities and 61 of 79 worked plots were improved, with Moscow at 17 of 19. The autoplay branches naturally diverged after the behavior change, so these are diagnostic comparisons rather than identical-game snapshots, but the Worker-count floor itself is directly visible and the reduction in under-improved worked land is large.
 
 This is therefore both a regression fix and a deliberate AI-strength enhancement, while also addressing a broader symptom that appears to have existed in Base AdvCiv already. Workers convert population and territory into food, production, commerce, routes and cleared terrain, so chronically running near one Worker per city can leave otherwise strong AI empires unable to exploit their own land. Further reserve/ratio tuning can still be revisited empirically, but the previous pathological shortage is considered fixed/improved for now.

