+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6094+) Update 3: the pre-war contact-distance guard is now substantially less restrictive and XML-tunable at a current, to address the issue, for example in save file 459, AIs having an overwhelming military advantage but not attacking much weaker rivals. The previous `SAS_UWAI_WAR_TARGET_MAX_LAND_CONTACT_TURNS` 3-turn land limit could hard-reject otherwise worthwhile reachable wars before normal UWAI evaluation simply because the army's estimated path to the nearest target city took slightly longer. To fix this, `SAS_UWAI_WAR_TARGET_MAX_LAND_CONTACT_TURNS` / `SAS_UWAI_WAR_TARGET_MAX_SEA_CONTACT_TURNS` are now changed to much higher defaults of 15 (vs 3 before) approximate path turns for land-reachable wars and 20 (vs 12 before to match this change too) for genuinely naval wars. The wider limits preserve a sanity bound for very remote targets while allowing ordinary UWAI power, utility, diplomacy, closeness and war-state logic to decide whether a reachable target is actually worth attacking. See [KI#61](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#61---seemingly-tremendously-improved-ais-getting-baited-to-attack-faraway-rivals-leaving-their-core-cities-mostly-defenseless-and-oftentimes-dying-shortly-after-to-an-opportunistic-rivals-changed-by-adding-a-pre-filter-reject-in-war-risk-assessment-based-on-distance-to-us-in-riskpreevaluate-in-warutilityaspectcpp).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6035+) **UWAI target choice avoids faraway wars:** Following [KI#61](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#61---seemingly-tremendously-improved-ais-getting-baited-to-attack-faraway-rivals-leaving-their-core-cities-mostly-defenseless-but-and-oftentimes-dying-shortly-after-to-an-opportunistic-rivals-changed-by-adding-a-pre-filter-reject-in-war-risk-assessment-based-on-distance-to-us-in-riskpreevaluate-in-warutilityaspectcpp)'s related fix, BBAI war-target logging in save file 450 showed the AI could still prepare some avoidable faraway wars: for example, on turn 182, Arabia (Team 0) could ignore closer Holy Rome (Team 6) at distance 5, attitudeValue -2, and targetPowerPercent 64, then prepare against the farther Netherlands (Team 5) at distance 10, attitudeValue +1, and targetPowerPercent 92. That is dangerous because a faraway war can split armies from the core, slow reinforcements, expose cities to opportunistic invasions, or make captures harder to hold. The new guard only optionally promotes a nearby land-war target, not an island/naval target, when it is close enough, weak enough, disliked/neutral enough, and has minimal UWAI drive; With the current distance threshold, it only steps in when the alternative target is at least 2 tiles farther away; targets at the same distance or closer are left to normal UWAI. This is intentionally a narrow blunder-fix rather than a war-logic overhaul, so UWAI choices do not become too bland or broadly overridden. In the confirming BBAI run, the guard found 32 valid closer targets and blocked 17 farther alternatives, leaving usual UWAI logic to either pick these closer targets or continue normally if no suitable target remained. See [KI#182](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#182---fixedimproved-uwai-war-target-selection-could-fall-through-to-farther-targets-even-when-a-closer-weakdisliked-land-target-was-available).
 - **Early-game razing — distance-aware captures**: Updated conquer/raze logic so that, for roughly the first **~120 turns** (scaled by game speed), the AI **razes captured cities** that aren’t **close enough** to justify early investment, in `CvPlayerAI::AI_conquerCity`. This addresses the base AdvCiv pattern where forces overextend (most troops sit in a faraway capture while **core cities** thin out) and also preserves scarce early economy (city maintenance, low pop, time to build improvements/culture). Indirect effect: less fog-busting far from us → **more barbarians** there, which tends to pressure rivals. Net: a **stronger, more focused** early AI (fewer "split in two" empires). See [KI#64](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#64---greatly-enhanced-ai-not-razing-faraway-cities-at-captures---now-we-raze-them-if-we-are-in-the-early-game-and-they-are-not-close-enough-thanks-to-changes-and-tweaks-in-cvplayeraiai_conquercity).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6042+) **Captured-city razing avoids destroy-then-resettle waste:** BBAI testing in save file 450 showed AIs repeatedly destroy a city and then soon spend a Settler to rebuild on the exact same `(38,50)` plot: Ur was razed, Tours was founded there and razed, then HRE founded Augsburg there only four turns later and eventually razed it too. This wasted population, buildings, Settlers, regrowth, and military effort. The cause was Base AdvCiv's `AI_isAwfulSite`, which misevaluated and judged existing cities through a separate crude terrain veto that could conflict with how Settlers valued the same site. AdvCiv-SAS replaces it with `AI_isSASCityLikelyToBenefitUsLongTerm`, whose intrinsic-quality component reuses the shared SAS low-food and potential-yield helpers: close cities with enough long-term food or workable potential can be kept even without standout bonuses. In the confirming run, HRE kept Ur when it captured the city at population 3; Ur later reached population 13, built a corporation building, and generated a Great Engineer, supporting a stronger AI outcome instead of another wasteful raze/resettle cycle. See [KI#186](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#186---fixedimproved-base-advciv-ai_isawfulsite-could-cause-economically-wasteful-captured-city-razeresettle-cycles-and-destroy-valuable-population-and-buildings).
